.TH ID 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
id \- print the user and group identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B id
.RB { \-u | \-g | \-G }
.RB [ \-nr ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B id
.RI [ user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B id
prints the user id and group id of the current user, or of
.I user
when one is given.
Without options it prints the user id, the group id, and the groups,
each with its name, and with
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-g ,
or
.B \-G
only the id that option selects.
.PP
Windows has no Unix user and group ids, so busybox supplies fixed ones:
.IP \(bu 2
The current user is user 4095 in group 4095, whoever the account is.
.IP \(bu 2
A process that is elevated and has the Administrators group enabled is
instead user 0 in group 0, both named
.BR root .
.IP \(bu 2
There are no supplementary groups, and no difference between real and
effective ids.
.PP
The user's name is the one the Windows function
.B GetUserName
returns, without a domain, with each space replaced by an underscore.
The group has the same name.
.PP
.I user
is a name, never a number.
Only two names are known: the current user's name, spelled as above,
which is user 4095 even in an elevated process, and
.BR root ,
which is user 0.
The current user's name must match exactly, case included.
Any other name fails.
.B id
takes at most one
.IR user .
.SS Output
Without options,
.B id
prints one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
uid=4095(name) gid=4095(name) groups=4095(name)
.EE
.RE
.PP
A name that is not known is left out of this line, with its parentheses.
With
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-g ,
or
.BR \-G ,
the line holds only the id, as a number or, with
.BR \-n ,
as a name.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-rGn ,
and may follow
.IR user .
.B \-\-
ends the options.
At most one of
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-g ,
and
.B \-G
is allowed, and
.B \-n
and
.B \-r
are allowed only with one of them.
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
there are no long options.
.TP
.B \-u
Print only the user id.
.TP
.B \-g
Print only the group id.
.TP
.B \-G
Print the ids of all the groups, separated by spaces.
There is only one, so the output is that of
.BR \-g .
.TP
.B \-n
Print names instead of numbers.
If the name is not known, print the number, report the error, and exit
with status 1.
.TP
.B \-r
Print the real id instead of the effective id.
Windows makes no difference, so it changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
The C library's option parser reads it.
When it is set, even to an empty value, options are recognized only
before the first operand.
So
.B id root \-u
is then an error, since
.B \-u
is a second operand.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error: an invalid command line, with a usage message on standard
error, an unknown
.IR user ,
a name that is not known for an id printed as a name, or a failure to
write standard output.
.PP
An unknown
.I user
is reported on standard error, as in
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ id bob
id: unknown user bob
.EE
.RE
.PP
An id that
.B \-n
prints as a name, but has none, is printed as a number instead, and
.B id: unknown ID
.I number
goes to standard error.
This happens only when Windows does not supply the user name.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the identity of the current user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ id
uid=4095(alice) gid=4095(alice) groups=4095(alice)
.EE
.RE
.PP
In an elevated process, such as a shell started by
.BR su :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ id
uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Do something only when elevated:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ "$(id \-u)" = 0 ] && echo elevated
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the name of the current user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ id \-un
alice
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
All users get the same numbers, so
.B id
does not tell users apart.
It does not report Windows security identifiers or group memberships.
The Administrators group only decides whether the process is
.BR root .
A command started by
.BR drop ,
.BR cdrop ,
or
.B pdrop
runs with reduced privileges and is not
.BR root ,
even from an elevated process.
.PP
.B whoami
prints the same name as
.BR "id \-un" ,
and
.B groups
prints what
.B id \-Gn
prints.
.B logname
prints the user's name even when elevated.
The name does not come from
.BR USER ,
.BR USERNAME ,
or
.BR LOGNAME .
.PP
Differences from GNU
.BR id :
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-z ,
or
.BR \-Z ,
and no long option other than
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
The operand is one name, never a numeric id.
.PP
The user name is read in the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows
10 version 1903 and later.
When standard output is a console, the output is converted from that
code page for display.
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR groups (1),
.BR logname (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR whoami (1)
